  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 117 PM CDT Thu Sep 3 2026\n\nRest of Today through Friday: This mornings convection has largely \npushed east of the region. There are a few clusters of \nshowers/storms redeveloping over western WI, just east of the Twin \nCities along a nearly stationary warm front. These storms may drift \nnorth and east into central and northern WI this afternoon, but with \nlimited instability and fairly weak upper level flow don't expect \nmush storm organization keeping the severe risk low through the \nafternoon. \n\nThe severe storm risk does increase late this evening and overnight \n(4-11Z) as an MCS, currently tracking into far northwest MN, moves \neast across the forecast area. CAMs are coming into decent agreement \nthat this MCS will track along a warm front/instability gradient \ndraped across northern MN and into north-central WI. Primary hazard \nwill be damaging wind gusts 50-60mph along with periods of heavy \nrain. One aspect that remains uncertain is how far east the severe \nwind risk will persist overnight as instability will be waning which \nmay result this system becoming elevated above a stable boundary \nlayer. One factor does does point to some storm maintains is an \nincreasing LLJ nosing into north-central WI resulting in locally \nenhanced low-level shear. CAMs generally show convection exiting the \narea to the east early Friday morning leaving the rest of the day \nmostly dry. There are some hints that a few isolated showers/storms \ncould develop Friday afternoon during peak heating as a weak \nshortwave moves across the region, however, the risk for stronger \nstorms expected moreso over southern where instability is expected \nto be greater.\n\nThis Weekend into Next Week: Warm and humid summertime air mass will \ncontinue to dominate the region through this weekend. While much of \nthe weekend should be dry, there will be periodic chances for shower \nand storms with any weak short-waves that pass over the region. \nGiven the subtle nature of the forcing confidence in the timing an \nlocation of any storms this weekend is low. Beyond this weekend \nthere is a 50-60% chance for showers/storms next Tuesday and \nWednesday with a cold frontal passage.",
